**Why this helps:** The chain constructor places transactions that unblock hot keys early, producing schedules whose per-key conflict orders are near-optimal — the main driver of makespan. ILS then only needs small local repairs instead of discovering structure from scratch. Randomized tie-breaking gives diverse structural starts, and the 50/50 restart mix keeps the random-greedy exploration as backup. All candidates are still validated via `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, and the ready-set merge avoids deadlock since it never enforces a strict chain (any transaction becomes placeable once it wins the priority contest).
